# Engineering Journal

## 2026-09-22 — FP viewmodel: continuous re-seat; dome stars; permanent mark

- **The FP "gun never faces the crosshair" bug** (companion to the TP entry
  below): `SeatGripInHand` aligned the gun to the camera ONCE, latched
  `SeatQuality.Final`, and the arms' idle clip then swung the welded hand —
  and gun — tens of degrees off. The seat was only ever correct for the frame
  it was measured on. Fix: re-seat every canonical frame (the
  `IsSeatReferenceValid` gate — no sprint, |pitch| ≤ 9°, phase Idle — keeps
  sprint tuck, recoil and aim pitch animating through the weld). `SeatGrip`
  gained a `verbose` flag so the per-frame path doesn't spam [Seat] logs.
- **mac10 corrections are ZERO** — see the TP entry: the mesh was measured
  (part-AABB probe + axis-gizmo renders) and follows the pack's +X-bore
  convention; the earlier FpRot/TpRot (0,90,0) values were compensations for
  the latch-drift bug and became the error once the real cause was fixed.
- **Harness gotcha that cost an hour**: a `Frozen = true` player never runs
  `AnimateFpGun`/`SeatGripInHand` (they live in the physics tick), so FP
  viewmodel captures show NO arms and NO gun. FP harnesses must spawn
  unfrozen players.
- **Arena dome stars invisible ONLINE only**: `VisualFlair.ApplyEnvironment`
  (called only on the online path) enables exponential depth fog, which
  integrates to a solid wall at the sky's infinite depth. `FogSkyAffect = 0`
  keeps the corridor haze but leaves the night-sky panorama clean.
- **Claim mark is PERMANENT** (owner's call): `WallhackDuration` is now
  +infinity — the hunted player stays marked (inflated red shell + diamond)
  until match teardown. The diamond's bob runs on its own phase accumulator
  because sin(infinite timer) is NaN.

## 2026-09-22 — FP viewmodel MAC-10 pointed straight up: SeatGrip discarded FpRot

- **Symptom**: the LOCAL player's own FP viewmodel (not the TP/puppet gun,
  already fixed above) rendered "wonky/sideways" — worst on the MAC-10, the
  spawn starter gun, so every match opened with a busted viewmodel.
- **Root cause**: `SeatGrip` (PlayerController.cs) unconditionally assumes a
  gun's bore is along local +X and rebuilds `model.Basis` from scratch against
  that assumption — silently discarding `GunDef.FpRot`, which existed
  precisely as the per-gun escape hatch for an asset whose axes don't match
  ("per-gun tweaks go in GunDef.FpRot", a comment that stopped being true the
  moment the seat system was added). The MAC-10 Styloo asset's bore is NOT
  along local +X like the other six guns; SeatGrip happily aligned the WRONG
  local axis to the camera's forward vector and reported `dot=1.000` — a
  perfectly-verified alignment of the wrong axis, so the log looked healthy
  while the muzzle rendered pointing at the ceiling. Confirmed via
  `test/TestTask.cs` (TestScope-style, no camera of its own): reproduced the
  arena's spawn double-equip AND a late re-equip of the same gun — both broke
  identically, ruling out a timing/race cause and pointing at the asset axis.
  ak47/awp/shotgun (equipped the same way, same code path) rendered correctly
  throughout, confirming this is per-asset, not systemic-timing.
- **Fix**: `SeatGrip` now takes a `localFwdRotDeg` correction (folded in via
  `Basis.FromEuler(...).Inverse()` composed with the existing world-basis
  math) and the FP call site (`SeatGripInHand`) passes `GunDef.FpRot`. Zero
  for every already-correct gun (identity rotation, no behaviour change —
  verified ak47/awp unaffected). `mac10.FpRot` set to `(0,90,0)`, found by
  sweeping candidates (same "measured not derived" method as `TpHandRot`,
  since asset axes aren't documented) and confirmed level + forward-facing by
  capture, in both the double-equip and late-equip cases.
- **TP intentionally NOT touched**: `SeatTpGrip`'s call site passes
  `Vector3.Zero` instead of `def.TpRot`, preserving that path's math
  bit-for-bit. Note for later: the TP fix above verified mac10 by `dot=1.000`
  only (see previous entry) — the same wrong-axis-verified-as-"correct" trap
  likely means the OPPONENT still sees a sideways MAC-10 in third person.
  Separate fix, out of scope here.
- Pre-existing, unrelated, left alone: the Sidearm (pew) FP viewmodel is
  nearly invisible at hip idle (a thin sliver between the hands) — looks like
  scale/occlusion by the oversized placeholder hand mesh, not an orientation
  bug (FpRot=0, unaffected by this change).

