## 2026-09-22 — Room + Pit doorways now meet the corridor arches (no more seeing outside)

- **Symptom**: standing in a hallway looking at a big Room, a sliver of the void was
  visible at the top of the arch.
- **Root cause, measured not guessed**: a corridor opening is 6 m wide and rises
  0..`CellHeight` (14 m) flat then arches to `FloorHeight` (18 m). A Room doorway was
  a flat-topped hole `RoomDoorH` (11 m) tall, and the panel sealing 11..18 m faced
  INTO the room. The room's own vault peaks at `RoomPeakY` **17.5 m — half a metre
  below the corridor's 18 m arch peak**. So a lens-shaped sliver of corridor arch sat
  above the room's ceiling with only a backface-culled panel in the way: from the
  corridor you looked straight through it and out of the level.
  - Where the lens exists, quantitatively: corridor arch `14 + 16t(1-t)` vs room
    vault `17.5 - 6.5((x-45)/15)²` — they cross around x=44 and x=46, i.e. a ~3 m
    wide, ~0.5 m tall lens at the crown. That is exactly the shape that showed up.
- **The Pit did NOT leak** and it is worth knowing why: `FloorMin=-3/FloorMax=3`
  means a pit always spans 3 floors, so on the entry floor its "ceiling" is just the
  next floor level at exactly 18 m — the same height the corridor arch peaks at. Only
  a pit's TOP floor has a real vault cap, and that one had the same latent lens.
- **Fix** — `RoomBuilder.ArchedDoorHead`, shared by both builders:
  - The doorway head now follows the CORRIDOR's own vault profile
    (`DungeonBuilder.VaultY`), tessellated into the same number of steps over the
    same span, so it meets the corridor's vault chords vertex-for-vertex.
  - Clipped to `min(corridor arch, this space's ceiling)`, so where the room's
    ceiling is lower the opening stops there instead of opening into nothing.
  - The strip above the head goes into a SECOND surface wound outward
    (`WallRoomSeal_*` / `WallPitSeal_*`). **This is the part that actually fixes the
    hole**: that strip is seen from the CORRIDOR, not from inside the room.
- **Three traps hit while doing it, all worth remembering:**
  1. Do NOT double-wind a quad into one SurfaceTool. `DungeonBuilder.Commit` calls
     `GenerateNormals`, which averages normals across coincident vertices in a
     surface — the two windings cancel to a zero normal and light black. Separate
     meshes; they cannot z-fight because exactly one is ever front-facing.
  2. Clipping to the ANALYTIC ceiling curve still leaked (6 px/frame): vaults render
     as chords that sag a few mm under the true parabola, so the head kissed the
     curve but not the mesh. `HeadCeilMargin = 0.12` sinks the head so the wall
     overlaps the ceiling. Applied ONLY to the ceiling clip — where the corridor arch
     is the limit the head must stay exact.
  3. The flanking wall beside a doorway must be split at the head's jamb height
     (`DoorJambY`), not at `RoomSpringY`. Otherwise the head's end vertex lands
     mid-edge on the flank: a T-junction that rasterises as a 1 px crack.
- **Verified** with `test/TestRoomArchSeal.tscn`: the world background is set to
  BRIGHT MAGENTA and the interior is fully enclosed, so any magenta pixel is a hole
  out of the level. 36-frame sweep per case (3 stand-off distances x 4 look angles x
  3 lateral offsets) rather than hand-picked framings.

  | case | what it stresses | void px before | after |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | room | 17.5 m ceiling vs 18 m arch — the original bug | 1224 worst frame | 4 |
  | roomside | vault SPRINGS at the doorway: 11 m under an 18 m arch | — | 6 |
  | roomL | L-group, entry off the bounding-box centre | — | 4 |
  | pit | shaft entry on a middle floor | 0 | 4 |
  | pittop | shaft entry on the TOP floor (real vault cap) | — | 4 |
  | **corridor (control)** | **no room, no pit at all** | — | **8** |

  **The control is the point**: a corridor-only maze leaks MORE (8 px) than any
  room/pit case, so the 4-6 px remaining are a pre-existing corridor vault seam, not
  the doorway seal. The junction-specific leak is gone.
- **Pre-existing, NOT fixed**: that corridor-only 1-2 px seam (worst when looking
  steeply up at a corridor vault). Out of scope here; invisible against the game's
  black background, but it is real if anyone chases it later.
- `TestRoomPitLogic` and `TestRoomPitConnectivity` still fully pass, including
  "room perimeter wall reaches within 0.1 m of FloorHeight".

## 2026-09-22 — Live population counter under Play Game

- **DEPLOYED 2026-09-22** as `53ece79` on `origin/main` — verified live, not
  assumed: probed `wss://palisade-relay.onrender.com` before (no reply) and
  after (`{"type":"stats","online":1,"lobby":0}`), which also confirms Render
  auto-deploys from `main`. The commit was built with git plumbing
  (`read-tree`/`commit-tree` against a throwaway `GIT_INDEX_FILE`) rather than
  by checking out a branch, because the working tree had ~350 uncommitted files
  including another session's in-flight refactor — so the commit contains ONLY
  `server/relay.js` and nothing was stashed, switched, or swept up.
- **Relay change was the enabling half.** The relay already tracked both
  numbers (`lobby` map, `wss.clients`) but never exposed them outside the
  lobby, so this adds a `stats` request/reply. Until `server/relay.js` ships to
  Render the counter stays hidden — an unknown message type falls through to
  the peer-forward branch, finds no room, and returns silently, so the client
  gets no answer. **"No answer" is rendered as nothing, never as "0 ONLINE"**,
  which would be a lie rather than a blank.
- **Asking does NOT join the lobby.** The obvious shortcut — have the menu send
  `lobby_join` and count the `lobby_update` it gets back — would list you as
  challengeable while you sit on the main menu, so people would challenge a
  player who is not actually looking. `stats` is read-only and leaves `lobby`
  untouched.
- **Reuses NetworkManager's socket** rather than opening a second one.
  `RequestStats()` only reconnects when the socket is genuinely Closed, so
  polling every 5 s costs one text frame, not one connection per poll. Safe
  against the matchmaking flow because `OpenSocket()` already defensively
  closes any existing connection before dialling, and resets `_wasOpen` so no
  spurious `ConnectionFailed` fires.
- **"online" counts every socket, this menu included** — so it reads as "people
  with the game open", not "people in a match". Side effect worth knowing: a
  client idling on the title screen now holds an open WebSocket, which keeps
  the Render free instance warm (fewer cold starts) but does hold a connection.
- **Verified end to end**, not just by inspection: ran `server/relay.js`
  locally with two extra sockets (one idle, one `lobby_join`), and the client —
  which defaults to the local relay in debug builds — received
  `{"type":"stats","online":3,"lobby":1}` and rendered "3 ONLINE · 1 IN LOBBY".

## 2026-09-22 — Hitmarker + headshot ding on landed damage

- **Where they hook in**: `GunCombat.DeliverDamage` is the single funnel every
  hit takes — GunCombat's own hit resolution and `Rocket`'s splash both route
  through it — so one insertion point covers online opponents *and* training
  dummies, and it already carries the `headshot` bool that drives the gold
  damage number. Files are `assets/audio/sfx/combat/hitmarker.mp3` and
  `headshotding.mp3` (kept at the names/extension Abe dropped in; renaming
  would orphan the `.import` files beside them).
- **Non-positional on purpose.** The existing `swordfleshimpact` call in the
  same method is an `AudioStreamPlayer3D` at the wound — it fades over 35 m and
  is meant for everyone nearby. The confirm sounds are `AudioStreamPlayer`
  (2D), so they land at full volume whether the target was 3 m or 80 m away.
  Replacing the 3D one instead would have meant your opponent hears your
  hitmarker.
- **A headshot stacks the ding ON TOP of the marker** rather than replacing it,
  so the confirmation always arrives in the same place and the ding reads as a
  bonus — swapping them makes a headshot feel like a *different* event instead
  of a better one.
- **Gated on `OnRemotePuppet`.** Puppets never resolve their own hits today, so
  this is belt and braces, but the audio is non-positional: if that ever
  changed you would hear YOUR hitmarker when the opponent shot YOU. Confirmed
  correct by auditing all three `TakeDamage` callers — incoming relayed damage
  (`OnlineDungeonArena:864`) never touches `DeliverDamage`, so getting hit
  stays silent.
- **Verified with real audio, not just a flag.** `test/TestHitMarker.cs` asserts
  the streams actually loaded (the null-guarded loader degrades a missing file
  to silence — exactly how every gun WAV was mute for everyone once already),
  that a landed body shot rings the marker and NOT the ding, and that a
  headshot rings both. `--write-movie` also records the mix to a `.wav`, so the
  run additionally proves ~199 KB of non-zero samples were emitted.
- **Visual hitmarker, built into `SpreadCrosshair` rather than its own Control.**
  Four ticks flicking outward from the reticle, ~0.32 s, white for a body hit and
  gold for a headshot to match the gold damage numbers `SpawnDamageNumber`
  already throws. It lives in that class because the class already resolves the
  gun and already computes `ReticlePos()` — and in third person the camera sits
  BEHIND the player, so a marker pinned to screen centre would sit off the
  target. Riding `ReticlePos` gets that right for free, and `_Process` already
  redraws every frame so the fade costs nothing. Drawn BEFORE the `IsAiming`
  early-out, because the AWP is exactly when you most need hit confirmation and
  the scope overlay suppresses the spread cross.
- **One event feeds both.** `GunCombat.OnHitConfirmed(bool headshot)` fires at
  the same instant and behind the same `OnRemotePuppet` gate as the audio, so
  the sound and the marker can never disagree.
- **Gotcha that produced a FALSE PASS: `dotnet build` silently skipped a file.**
  A test rewritten via heredoc had an mtime in the same second as the previous
  build output, MSBuild's incremental check considered it up to date, reported
  "0 Errors", and Godot ran the STALE assembly — printing the old test's
  assertions, all passing. It was only caught because the printed strings did
  not match the source on disk. After writing a file from the shell, `touch` it
  (or verify a known-new string appears in the output) before trusting a run.
- **Found en route: `test/TestGunRange.cs` target geometry is wrong.** It builds
  its dummy with the capsule offset `+0.9` and the body origin at the feet, but
  `GunCombat` computes `footY = GlobalPosition.Y - 0.8` (capsule centred on the
  origin). Every "centre mass" shot in that harness therefore lands at
  `relY ≈ 2.4`, past `HeadMinY` 1.35, and scores a 1.5× HEADSHOT — so its
  published TTK table is ~1.5× too fast. Not fixed here; flagged.

