## 2026-09-15 — Main menu: a "CORNERS" wordmark built from maze tiles, orbitable

- **What**: the 108 pt Label title is gone. `scripts/TitleWordmark.cs` spells
  CORNERS out of 117 **Open Room pieces** — the full-cell slabs the map editor
  draws (`BuildPieceInto`'s `PieceType.Room` branch) plus cutaway walls on each
  letter's outer boundary, reusing `AddRoomCutawayWalls`'s rule that a face
  gets a wall only where the neighbouring cell is empty. Normals point INWARD
  with `cull_back`, so near walls drop out and you see into the letters as you
  orbit. Laid flat on XZ like a floor plan.
- **Pieces are editor CORRIDORS, not plain squares.** Each cell draws the
  editor's `CentW` central square plus an `ArmW × ArmLen` arm toward every
  connected neighbour, walled by `AddCutawayWalls`'s arrangement (side walls
  along each open arm, caps on closed directions). An "opening" is just a
  neighbouring cell that is also part of the glyph, so every letter comes out
  as a real corridor network shaped like the letter. Consequence: connected
  cells merge into continuous runs, so per-cell seams give way to corridor
  structure — pieces are inset 0.4 rather than the editor's 0.1 hairline so
  the seams still read at menu scale.
- **The corridor proportions are deliberately fatter than the editor's.** At
  the editor's own `CentW = CS*0.50` / `ArmW = CS*0.40` the letters came out
  as thin broken outlines: those numbers are tuned for a 10 m hallway a player
  runs down, and these glyphs have SINGLE-CELL strokes, so the carve-out ate
  roughly half of every stroke and the wordmark read thinner than the solid
  tiles it replaced. `CS*0.70` / `CS*0.62` keeps the shape language (corner
  notches, capped dead ends, arms only toward connected neighbours) with
  strokes solid enough to read. If the glyphs ever go to 2-cell strokes, the
  editor's original proportions become viable again.
  And the colour is a **starry night sky** instead of jade, matched to a
  reference image Abe supplied: vivid cerulean nebula with hot blue-white
  cores over a navy void, under a dense white-cyan starfield. A procedural
  shader (unshaded, like the editor's tiles, so it needs no lights) keyed off
  **world** XZ, so the sky is continuous across the whole word instead of
  restarting on every tile. It animates off `TIME`: stars drift and twinkle
  (each with its own size and phase, so the field never pulses in unison)
  while the nebula creeps the opposite way. Stars are deliberately NOT
  occluded by the nebula — in the reference they sit over the bright blue.
- **Two legibility guards the reference itself doesn't need.** A full-frame sky
  can go black in places; this one only shows INSIDE the letters, against a
  black page. So (a) stars are gated on the world normal's Y and land only on
  up-facing tops — world XZ is constant down a vertical face, so a star on a
  cutaway wall smears into a bright vertical stripe; and (b) the letter sides
  use a FLAT dim-blue `edge_col` rather than a dimmed copy of the sky above
  them, because a dimmed dark patch leaves the silhouette invisible. Note the sky is deliberately MOONLIT, not
  midnight: a genuinely dark one sinks into the black background and kills the
  contrast that makes the grid seams visible — the first pass had `sky_deep` at
  0.09/0.13/0.26 and the left C started disappearing.
- **Lives under the Control root, no SubViewport.** The root viewport renders
  its World3D *behind* the 2D canvas, so the title screen's full-rect black
  `ColorRect` had to GO — it hid the 3D completely. The wordmark's own
  Environment paints the black now.
- **The drag plumbing is all `MouseFilter`**, and it is easy to get silently
  wrong: a bare `Control` defaults to `Stop`, so the full-expand spacers, the
  layout containers AND — the one that actually broke it — the `TitleScreen`
  root itself each swallow background clicks before `_UnhandledInput` sees
  them. All are `Ignore` now; `MouseFilter` is per-node, so the menu Buttons
  still capture their own clicks. `test/TestTitleDrag.cs` pushes a real drag
  through `Viewport.PushInput` (which runs the true GUI-pick → unhandled
  chain) and asserts the wordmark orbits, plus a negative case proving a press
  on a Button does not orbit. Reasoning alone had me convinced this worked
  when it did not — the root Control was missed until the test failed.
- **Drag orbits it**, same gesture and sensitivity as the maze editor
  (0.005 rad/px, wheel zoom). Double-click resets the framing.
- **Orbit the WORD, not a lowered target.** The first attempt aimed the camera
  at a point 175 units below the word to push it up-screen — but that moves the
  camera too, so camera-to-word was never `_dist`: at top-down the camera sat
  only ~164 units above the tiles and the word overflowed the frame, and near
  edge-on it sank BELOW the tile plane while still aiming down, so the word left
  the screen entirely. The screen lift now comes from re-aiming along the
  camera's OWN up axis (`0.22 × dist`) without moving it, which is pitch- and
  zoom-independent: a constant ~178 px of a 720-tall frame.
- **Default pose: bigger, tilted, chunkier.** `DefDist` 470 → 340 (the word
  spans ~1045 px, 82% of a 1280 frame, up from 744), `FlrT` 0.55 → 1.6 with
  4.2-high corridor walls so the pieces read as solid blocks at an angle, and
  `DefPitch` 0.95 → 0.78. Tilt is a straight trade against legibility: vertical
  extent scales with `sin(pitch)`, so 0.62 squashed the 7-row glyphs to ~4
  rows' worth and the carved corridors stopped reading as letters. 0.78 keeps
  ~86% of the upright height (vs 71% at 0.62) while still being clearly more
  tilted than 0.95. `MaxYaw` tightened 0.20 → 0.15 to match the wider word —
  verified at the new pitch, the turned word spans y≈40–280 against a menu at
  317.
- **Every clamp has a measured reason.** Pitch `0.40..1.25`: too flat and the word
  squashed to an illegible sliver, and near 1.5 rad the `+Y` up hint runs
  nearly parallel to the view direction, `LookAt` degenerates, and the word
  rendered **mirrored** ("SЯƎNЯOϽ"). Yaw `±0.20`: a wide word sweeps
  vertically by `sin(yaw) × width`, so the bigger the word the tighter this
  must be — at ±0.28 the enlarged word's low corner reached the menu column.
  Dist `330..800`: the word scales as `DefDist/dist`, and below ~330 it spans
  the full 1280 and shaves the C and S off the edges. **This floor is tied to
  DefDist — resizing the word means moving it too.**
- **Verified**: `test/TestTitleWordmark.cs` films all seven poses the mouse can
  actually reach. Captures confirm the default framing (word ~930 px, 73% of
  width, y≈90–220, menu clear at y≈317), legibility at both pitch limits, no
  mirroring anywhere in range, and no crop at full zoom-in.

