## 2026-08-31 — Third person: camera pitches, reticle tracks the real shot

- **What**: In third person the camera could not look up or down, and the
  crosshair sat at screen centre — which in TP is the player's own back, not
  where the bullet goes. Both fixed.
- **Lock 1 — the camera never pitched.** `ArenaBuilder`-style orbit in
  `PlayerController` built its offset from yaw only:
  `back = (sin(_yaw), 0, cos(_yaw))`, plus a fixed `Vector3.Up * elev`, then
  `LookAt(pivot)`. Mouse Y moved `_pitch` (and so the aim ray) but nothing in
  that expression read `_pitch`, so the view was pinned to one height. Now
  orbits on both axes: `back = (sin(_yaw)*cos(p), -sin(p), cos(_yaw)*cos(p))`.
  **At pitch 0 the framing is byte-identical to before** — the change only adds
  the missing freedom.
- **`TpPitchClamp = 70°`, deliberately tighter than `PitchClamp = 85°.** At ±90°
  the orbit offset is parallel to `Vector3.Up`, which makes `LookAt`'s up-vector
  degenerate and NaNs the camera basis. Do not raise it to match PitchClamp.
- **Lock 2 — the reticle was pinned to screen centre.** `SpreadCrosshair._Draw`
  passed `Size / 2f` unconditionally. That is exactly right in FP, where the
  view ray IS the aim ray — but in TP the shot still leaves from the FP camera
  at the head while the visible camera sits behind and above, so the two rays
  diverge. **Measured: at level pitch the true aim point is 143px above screen
  centre.** That gap was the entire "aiming feels locked" complaint.
  `ReticlePos()` now traces the aim ray (`UpdateAimPoint`, TP only, so FP pays
  nothing) and unprojects the hit point through the active camera.
  `IsPositionBehind` guard first — behind the near plane `UnprojectPosition`
  mirrors the point to the wrong side of the screen.
- **Verified**: new `test/TestTpFreeLook.tscn` drives real
  `InputEventMouseMotion` through `PlayerController._Input` (needs
  `Input.MouseMode = Captured`, which is what gates mouse look) so the
  production path is what runs:

  | beat | pitch | camY − playerY | reticle | off-centre |
  |---|---|---|---|---|
  | level | 0.000 | 2.718 | (640, 217.0) | 143.0px |
  | up 40° | 0.695 | −1.125 | (640, 211.6) | 148.4px |
  | down 40° | −0.639 | 6.298 | (640, 324.4) | 35.6px |

  Camera drops when looking up, rises when looking down. FP reticle still lands
  at exactly (640, 360) = centre. Frames in `screenshots/tplook/`.
- **Test-design note**: the harness first added its own `PlayerHUD`, which was
  wrong twice over — a duplicate reticle, and it could have passed on a HUD the
  real game never builds. `PlayerController._Ready` already adds one as a direct
  child (line ~642), which is also what makes `HudLookup.FindPlayer`'s
  walk-up-the-tree lookup resolve. Removed it; verdict unchanged.
- **Not done — body/camera decoupling**: `_playerBody.Rotation` still tracks
  `_yaw` every frame, so the character always faces the camera. True free-look
  (orbit without turning the body) is a separate, larger change and was not part
  of this fix.

