## 2026-08-28 — Rifle animations authored in Blender

Goal: make weapon state readable between opponents. The pistol clips were being
used for every gun, and a long gun held at pistol grip width (~0.10 m, both hands
at the receiver) reads as *carried*, not *aimed* — and the rifle stayed horizontal
even when aiming up.

**Authored a rifle set in Blender** (`playerModels/animations/RifleSet.glb`, 909 KB,
6 clips) derived from the UAL1 pistol clips: `Rifle_Idle_Loop`, `Rifle_Aim_Up`,
`_Neutral`, `_Down`, `Rifle_Shoot`, `Rifle_Reload`.

Method that finally worked (take 3 — the first two were wrong and worth recording):
1. **Take 1** dragged only the LEFT hand onto a foregrip 26 cm forward. It barely
   moved (0.100 → 0.122 m): the pistol stance already holds the arms nearly
   straight out — shoulder-to-hand ~0.56 m against ~0.55 m of arm — so the target
   was simply **out of reach**.
2. **Take 2** derived both hand targets from the SHOULDERS. That discarded the
   clip's posed weapon plane and produced a shrug with the arms splayed sideways.
3. **Take 3 (kept)** *perturbs the pose that already works*: keep the pistol clip's
   hand position as the anchor, then slide the firing hand 17 cm BACK along the
   barrel axis and the support hand 13 cm FORWARD along it. The weapon stays where
   the animator put it and only the grip opens to ~0.21 m. Both targets start from
   a known-reachable point so the 2-bone IK barely works. Baked with
   `nla.bake(visual_keying, clear_constraints)`.

`Rifle_Reload` is a straight copy of the pistol reload — its left hand leaving the
weapon to fetch a magazine is exactly the tell we want an opponent to read.

**The barrel axis on this rig is the firing hand's local +Y** (measured, not
assumed).

**Wiring:** `PlayerAnimator`'s upper-body layer now holds TWO families in one
state machine (`GunAim`/`GunShoot`/`GunReload` and `RifleAim`/…), so switching
weapon class is a Travel with no tree rebuild. `GunDef.IsPistol` is true only for
`pew`; everything else is shouldered and uses the rifle set.

### Two bugs worth remembering

**1. Un-retargeted animation GLBs animate NOTHING, silently.** The rifle clips
imported with default settings kept raw track paths
(`Armature/Skeleton3D:pelvis`) instead of Godot's retargeted humanoid paths
(`%GeneralSkeleton:Hips`). They loaded with the correct length and track count and
produced no error — and moved no bones, giving a T-pose. Fixed by copying the
`_subresources` `"nodes"` block (the `retarget/bone_map`) from
`UAL1_Standard.glb.import`. `PlayerAnimator.DiagnoseClips` now fails loudly on this.

**2. `Travel()` on a nested state machine that has not started goes nowhere.**
Switching to the rifle family on equip silently did nothing because the equip fired
before the machine ran its first frame. The upper layer now re-asserts its aim node
every `Tick` instead of relying on call ordering.

### Blender access

The `blender` MCP server reports `✗ Failed to connect`, and its tools only load at
session start regardless — but the **companion server is not needed**. The add-on
itself listens on `127.0.0.1:9876` speaking null-byte-delimited JSON, so a plain
socket works. Helper kept at `tools_blend.ps1`. Two gotchas cost real time: the
payload and its null terminator must go in ONE write (otherwise the add-on evicts
you with "Client timed out", which looks like a hang), and `bpy.ops` needs a
`temp_override` with a real VIEW_3D window/area or `bpy.context.object` is None.

## 2026-08-28 — Gun pose was killing ALL locomotion (root cause + fix)

**Symptom:** equipping a gun froze the character's legs — walking, running,
jumping and wall-running all stopped animating.

**Root cause: a broken parameter path, not a blending problem.** Wrapping the
locomotion `AnimationNodeStateMachine` in the upper-body `AnimationNodeBlendTree`
moved it from the tree root to a nested node, so its playback path changed from
`parameters/playback` to `parameters/LocoSM/playback`. `EnsurePlayback` still read
the old path, got null, and returned early — which meant `PlayState` could never
`Travel()` the locomotion machine. Locomotion was stuck on whatever it started on,
forever. `UpdateLocoBlend` had the same problem with
`parameters/Locomotion/blend_position`.

Fixed with a `LocoParamPrefix` (`"LocoSM/"` when the upper layer is active, empty
otherwise) applied to both. The bone filter and blend weights were never wrong.

**Worth remembering:** nesting a state machine inside a blend tree silently
re-paths every one of its parameters. Nothing errors — `AnimationTree.Get` on a
bad path just returns null.

**Verification** (`test/TestThirdPerson.tscn`, extended): the harness now drives
the full locomotion set, not just speed — `DebugDriveThirdPerson` takes
`onFloor` / `sliding` / `wallRunning` / `verticalVel` per beat, and the beat list
leads with IDLE, WALKING, RUNNING, JUMPING, FALLING, WALL RUN, SLIDING before the
weapon beats. Captured and read: walking shows a clear mid-stride, jumping shows
knees tucked airborne, wall-run shows stride plus lean — all with the rifle held
in both hands throughout. Legs and arms genuinely combine.

