## Weapon pickup: radial prompt + offline claim fix

Ask: "make the hold-E to pick up a circle like Roblox, make it pick up way
faster, and put the E symbol on the weapon."

### The premise was wrong, and that mattered

There was no hold. Pickup was `Input.IsActionJustPressed("interact")` — a single
instant press, the only `"interact"` consumer in the codebase. So "make it
faster" could not be about hold duration.

**The real cause: offline pickup was completely broken.** `WeaponPickup` fires
`ClaimAttempted`, and `OnlineDungeonArena` was its ONLY subscriber.
`DungeonArena` spawned the pickup and never subscribed, so in the test maze the
event went nowhere and `ConfirmPickup()` was unreachable — the arena weapon
could not be picked up at all. Fixed by subscribing in `DungeonArena`
(offline has no host to arbitrate, so the claim confirms itself).

### `scripts/ProximityPrompt.cs` (new)

Billboarded ring gauge with the key letter in the middle. Procedural shader —
no texture asset, crisp at any distance. Weapon-agnostic: it only renders a
0..1 `Progress`, so it is reusable for any interactable.

- `SetKeyFromAction("interact")` reads the ACTUAL bound key from `InputMap`.
  Interact is rebindable in SettingsPanel, so a hardcoded "E" would go stale.
- `depth_test_disabled` — the prompt sits on the weapon, so without it the
  weapon's own mesh punches holes through the ring.
- Fill is applied to the BAND only. The first version applied it across the
  whole quad, which tinted the inner disc: it read as a filling pie and washed
  out the letter. Caught by screenshot, not by the build.
- Ring is deliberately small (0.44m) and its disc only 34% opaque — a larger,
  more opaque badge hid the weapon it was advertising.

### Hold

`WeaponPickup.HoldSeconds` — now **0 (instant on press)** per follow-up. The
radial prompt still renders; at 0 it reads as a permanently full ring. Raise it
to reintroduce a hold; the rewind-on-early-release path is still there.

A latch (`_claimSent`) fires at most one claim per press. Necessary once
HoldSeconds is 0: the claim branch is then reached on EVERY frame the key is
down, and online each claim is a network round-trip to the host — a held key
would otherwise spam one message per frame.

Verified visually via `test/TestPickupPrompt.cs` (3 pickups at 0 / 0.45 / 0.85
fill). Regressions clean: `TestRoomPitLogic` RESULT OK, `TestAltarWalk` 12/12.

