## Dungeon: two-sided surfaces were shaded inside-out (dark stairs)

Playtest: "the stairs have pieces where they are too dark."

Not a lighting problem — the geometry was being shaded inside-out.

`TexSurfShaderSrc` overrides `NORMAL` in its fragment stage, computing it from
its own `wnorm` varying. `wnorm` carries the FRONT face's outward direction no
matter which side is being rasterised. Godot normally flips the built-in
`NORMAL` for back-facing fragments, but overriding it bypasses that. Stairs and
every `doubleSided` surface use `cull_disabled`, so their back faces were lit
with a normal pointing away from every light in the room, and rendered black.

Fix is one line in the fragment stage, before the tangent frame is built (T and
B derive from N, so this corrects those too):

    if (!FRONT_FACING) N = -N;

`StoneShaderSrc` is fine and was left alone — it only uses `abs(wnorm)` for
triplanar axis selection and never writes `NORMAL`, so Godot's automatic flip
still applies there. Checked rather than assumed.

**Measured A/B** on `StairInspect`, mean luminance and share of pure-black
pixels per frame:

| frame | mean off | mean on | delta | %black off | %black on |
|-------|----------|---------|-------|------------|-----------|
| 0-2   | 25.9-27.1| same    | +0.01 | ~3.1       | ~3.1      |
| 3     | 26.55    | 29.79   | +3.24 | 27.68      | 15.30     |
| 4     | 25.11    | 28.05   | +2.94 | 28.12      | 15.90     |

Black pixels roughly halve in the two views that actually look at back faces;
the three views that only see front faces are unchanged, which is the control.

**Gotcha:** the shader lives in a C# verbatim string (`@"..."`). The first
version of this comment contained double quotes around the playtest phrase and
silently terminated the shader source mid-file — three `CS1002` errors pointing
at a line nowhere near the real problem. No bare `"` in comments inside these
shader strings.

Regressions after the change: TierCheck PASS, TestRoomPitLogic RESULT OK,
Protrusion PASS.

