## Hallway modules v2: dungeon scale + detail pass + a renderer discovery

Rescaled all six hallway GLBs to the maze corridor contract (10m cell, 6m
opening, 14m walls — DungeonBuilder's CellSize/OpeningW/CellHeight) and ran the
requested detail pass. A 14m wall at reference proportions is mostly blank, so
each style distributes detail in three bands: functional 0-3m (doors, sconces,
wainscot), rhythm 3-11m (pilasters, panel grids, curtains, ivy), cornice 11-14m
(beams, coves, domes, garlands). Poly deltas: office 156→462, hotel 382→1662,
manor 348→2052, ocean 168→708, redlodge 1094→4128, garden 2656→5902.

### The real find: gl_compatibility drops omnis past ~32 total

Raising light rows for the tall ceilings produced an impossible-looking bug:
floors lit, ceilings 2.5m from a light rendered ambient-black. Bisection chain,
each step transplanting one element into a minimal probe:

  high omni + plate ........................ bright (not orientation)
  12-light row ............................. bright (not a small count cap)
  the real GLB ............................. bright (not asset/materials)
  Pack+Instantiate path .................... bright (not the load path)
  demo viewpoint ........................... bright (not view-dependent)
  54 lights, one mesh ...................... bright (!!)
  solo style in the REAL demo .............. bright
  full six styles, 56 lights ............... BLACK ceilings
  full demo, census halved to 28 ........... every ceiling lit

Conclusion: total omnis in frame must stay ≤ ~32 on this renderer;
`max_renderable_lights=512` does not lift it. The dropped set is arbitrary
enough that SOME surfaces keep light (floors) while others lose it (ceilings),
which is what made every "check one thing" probe pass. Recorded in auto-memory
(gl-compat-light-budget) — this constrains the arena too, and retroactively
justifies keeping the tier torch rings at 16+8+8.

HallwayDemo.cs now documents a hard 30-light census (3+7+5+5+5+5) next to the
style table, plus a `solo:<style>` bisect arg kept for future lighting work.

Residual: the garden vault cap still reads as shade rather than catching
visible pools; acceptable architectural read, revisit only if it grates.

