## Per-gun shot sounds; and the root cause of the sideways weapon

### Audio: seven guns, two samples, zero variation

`GunShotLight.wav` served mac10/pew and `GunShotHeavy.wav` served the other
five — an AWP and a quad rocket were literally the same noise. Worse,
`_fireSfx.Play()` had **no pitch variation**, so every round was a bit-identical
waveform; at the MAC-10's 0.15 s interval that phase-locks into a flat buzz
instead of reading as distinct rounds.

`tools/gunsfx_gen.py` (new, stdlib only) synthesises one shot per gun. A
gunshot is three stacked events and the proportions ARE the weapon's identity:
crack (1-8 ms, bright, carries perceived power), body (20-90 ms, carries
calibre), tail (0.1-0.6 s, carries space). Sniper is nearly all crack, shotgun
nearly all body, and the launchers get almost no crack at all — a rocket
leaving a tube is a whoosh and a low thump, which is why launchers so often
sound wrong when built from a rifle sample.

Tails use discrete early reflections (11/23/37/59 ms slap-back, high-damped)
because the game is hard stone corridors; a smooth exponential tail alone
sounds outdoors. Output matches the existing assets: 44.1 kHz mono 16-bit.

Added `FirePitchJitter = 0.04f` (+/-4%) per shot — under the threshold where a
listener hears "detuned", well over the one where repeats stop sounding
copy-pasted.

TestGunSlots now asserts every FireSfx resource loads and all seven are unique:
a typo'd path does not fail the build, the gun just fires silently.

**Not verified: how any of this SOUNDS.** Synthesised blind; the parameters are
all in the GUNS table at the bottom of the generator.

### Sideways weapon: root cause (systematic-debugging)

`PlayerController.SeatGrip` orients a weapon by baking a correction relative to
the HAND BONE's orientation at the instant of seating:
`model.Basis = holder.Basis⁻¹ * world`. It runs ONCE per equip
(`_gripSeated = true`, "one-shot either way"), 20 physics frames later so the
family aim-pose xfade can settle.

**The bug: nothing checks WHICH pose is active when that snapshot is taken.**
The hand bone is driven by an aim blendspace (`PlayerAnimator` line ~517:
"Vertical aim blend: -1 aiming down, 0 level, +1 aiming up. Driven by camera
pitch") and by sprint state (`<Fam>_Sprint_Loop`). So the reference frame is
whatever the arms happened to be doing at frame 20:

- looking level, standing → correct
- looking up/down → gun permanently canted by the aim-blend delta
- **sprinting → off by (aim − sprint carry)**, and per the weapon-families
  design those differ hugely (rocket "at-chest log carry" vs shoulder mount)

Every later frame is then `handPose_now * handPose_seat⁻¹`, which is exactly the
intended behaviour for small carry tilts and catastrophic for a whole different
pose. That is the "sometimes": it depends entirely on what the player was doing
during the third of a second after pickup or switch.

**The existing `[Seat] dot=` log cannot catch this** — it measures the barrel
immediately after assigning the basis, so it always reports dot≈1.000. It
verifies the arithmetic, not whether the reference pose was valid.

Two candidate hypotheses were investigated and DISPROVED first: a stale
`_fpSeatDelay` counter (it is reset, line 1902) and a missing rebuild on weapon
switch (`OnEquipped` rebuilds and resets both FP and TP seats).

**Newly urgent:** the 1/2/wheel switching added this session rebuilds the
viewmodel on every swap, so each mid-fight switch re-runs the one-shot seat —
usually while sprinting or looking off-level. Expect this to go from occasional
to frequent.

Fix not applied (diagnosis was what was asked). The shape of it: seat against a
canonical pose rather than the live one — either gate seating on
(not sprinting && |aim blend| ≈ 0), or drive the animator to a known aim-neutral
frame for the measurement, or re-seat whenever the family/sprint state changes
instead of once per equip.

## Fix: the sideways gun — seat against a canonical pose, self-correcting

Root cause (diagnosed previous entry): `SeatGrip` bakes a correction relative to
the HAND BONE's live orientation, and the old code ran it on a fixed 20-frame
timer with **no check on which pose was active**. Seating mid-sprint or at a
steep aim baked that pose in permanently — `_gripSeated = true` was one-shot.

### The fix

`SeatQuality { None, Provisional, Final }` replaces the one-shot bool, on both
the FP and TP seats:

- **Final** — measured while the pose was canonical. Never re-measured.
- **Provisional** — the settle window lapsed without a canonical frame (player
  sprinted off the spawn, say). Taken anyway so the weapon is not invisible,
  and **upgraded on the first canonical frame**.
- **None** — never measured; weapon hidden (unchanged behaviour).

Canonical is a pure, testable predicate — `IsSeatReferenceValid(sprinting,
pitchRadians, phase)`: not sprinting, |pitch| <= 9 deg, and `Phase.Idle`.
9 deg because `PlayerAnimator.SetAimPitch` maps +/-60 deg onto the full -1..1
aim blend, so that is a blend of 0.15 — near enough to neutral that the residual
is smaller than the idle sway the design deliberately keeps.

The key property: **a bad measurement is no longer permanent.** Stand still
looking roughly level for one frame and the weapon corrects itself. The
correction is still captured ONCE and held (not recomputed per frame), so
carry/reload tilts still read as hand-bone deltas exactly as designed —
recomputing continuously would pin the gun to camera-forward and kill the sway.

`_animSprinting` mirrors every `SetSprinting` call because `PlayerAnimator`
exposes no getter.

### Verification

`test/TestSeatGate.cs` (new) — 21 checks on the predicate: sprint rejected even
when level, steep aim rejected both ways, reload/fire rejected, a normal 5 deg
glance still accepted (a gate nobody satisfies is the bug in disguise), and
+/-symmetry across 0-30 deg (an asymmetric bound would seat fine looking up and
badly looking down — "still sometimes wrong"). **A/B'd: deleting the sprint term
fails the suite.**

Full sweep green: SeatGate, GunSlots, RocketHit, CombatLock, RoomPitLogic,
AltarWalk 12/12, PickupReach, Protrusion, TierCheck.

